❯ Write a 700-word essay about the history of terminal emulators. Do not use any tools.

● The History of Terminal Emulators

  The terminal emulator is one of computing's great survivors—a software artifact that persists not
  because it's optimal, but because it successfully abstracted away a piece of hardware that no
  longer exists. To understand terminal emulators, you have to start with the thing they're
  emulating: the physical computer terminal.

  In the 1960s and 1970s, mainframes and minicomputers were expensive, centralized resources. Users
  didn't have their own computers; they had terminals—devices with a keyboard and a display (or,
  earlier, a printer) that connected to a distant machine, often via serial cable or telephone line.
  The most influential of these was the DEC VT100, released in 1978. It displayed 24 lines of 80
  characters, supported cursor addressing, and introduced escape sequences—special character codes
  that told the terminal to move the cursor, clear the screen, or change text attributes. These
  escape sequences became a de facto standard, and software written to target the VT100 could run,
  unmodified, on any terminal that faithfully copied its behavior.

  This is the crucial turning point: once escape sequences became standardized enough, the physical
  terminal became replaceable by software that merely behaved like one. As personal computers
  proliferated in the 1980s, users still needed to connect to mainframes, universities, and bulletin
  board systems. Rather than buying dedicated terminal hardware, they ran terminal emulation






